<h2>A genuinely Bangladesh-anchored product</h2>
<p>Of every product line we've verified data for, masoor (red lentil) shows perhaps the most striking single-market concentration outside of a few outlier cases like DORB. Bangladesh took USD 91.5 million of India's USD 148.7 million total 2024 masoor exports — roughly 62% of both value and volume. If you're a Bangladesh-based buyer of Indian lentils, you're not a niche customer in a diversified trade; you're the trade.</p>

<h2>An important nuance: India isn't a pure surplus exporter here</h2>
<p>Worth understanding before assuming this is a simple "India grows more than it needs" story. India is simultaneously a significant lentil importer in its own right — duty-free import windows for certain pulses have run at various points to manage domestic supply gaps. What that means practically is that our position supplying masoor to Bangladesh reflects processing and sourcing capability, established trade relationships, and specific grade availability, not a straightforward domestic-surplus narrative. It's worth asking any Indian supplier how they source masoor for export, given this dynamic.</p>

<h2>Specification</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Form</strong> — whole or split (masoor dal)</li>
  <li><strong>Moisture</strong> — typically ≤12%</li>
  <li><strong>Admixture</strong> — foreign matter and defect percentage</li>
  <li><strong>Colour consistency</strong> — particularly relevant for retail-facing buyers</li>
</ul>

<h2>Logistics</h2>
<p>Both sea and land routing apply, as with most Bangladesh-bound trade. Sea via Chattogram (2-5 days) suits larger container volumes; land via Petrapole-Benapole (1-3 days) is often faster when sourcing from western India. See our <a href="/markets/bangladesh/">Bangladesh market guide</a> for the full picture on documentation and payment norms.</p>

<h2>Beyond Bangladesh</h2>
<p>Iraq is the clear second-largest market for Indian masoor in the data we've verified, at a much smaller scale, with Djibouti, the UAE, Malaysia and Sri Lanka rounding out meaningful volumes. If you're sourcing masoor for a market other than Bangladesh, tell us — availability and lead times can differ from the Bangladesh-focused supply chain most of our masoor sourcing is built around.</p>

<h2>Planning around India's own supply position</h2>
<p>Because India's own lentil production doesn't always cover domestic demand, masoor availability for export can tighten in years when Indian domestic prices rise and government policy responds by prioritising domestic supply. This isn't a constant risk, but it's a genuine one worth building into any long-term Bangladesh masoor programme — diversifying your supplier relationships and maintaining some flexibility in order timing gives you more room to manage a tighter-supply year without disruption to your own downstream business.</p>
